Now, read the following csharp code and output assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@".
```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);

            // @@@ index value is greater than or equal to 0 

            // @@@ index value is less than arrList.Count
            Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count);

            // @@@ index value is greater than or equal to 0 
            Debug.Assert(index >= 0);
        }
    }
}
```

Do note, that the input code isn't correct in terms of the use of the 'sum' variable within the 'PUT_GetContract' method's scope, as 'sum' is only defined within the 'Sum' method's scope. There're no corresponding 'Debug.Assert' for those two comments 'sum is greater than or equal to x and y' and 'index value is greater than or equal to 0'. In real-world scenarios, we should refactor and correct such errors before implementing assertion checks.--------------------
